<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dosunmu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Docemo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dosunmu, also spelt Docemo, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-of-lagos">Oba of Lagos</a> from 1853 to 1885, succeeding his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akitoye">Akitoye</a>. Under strong pressure from the British consul and a naval squadron, he signed the treaty of 6 August 1861 ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in return for a pension, and Lagos became a British colony. He kept his title and some ceremonial authority until his death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dosunmu was born around 1823. His accession broke with tradition, because he owed it to the British consul Benjamin Campbell, who had gained influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> after the Reduction of Lagos in December 1851. Learning of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akitoye">Akitoye</a>'s death on 2 September 1853, Campbell kept the news from the chiefs and first asked them who the heir should be. Once they named Dosunmu, the death was announced, hasty ceremonies followed, and the next day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> saluted the new oba with 21 guns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He inherited his father's rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosoko">Kosoko</a>, who held an independent base at Epe with about 400 warriors. Under the Treaty of Epe of 28 September 1854 Kosoko gave up his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, but the rivalry continued in trade: Dosunmu had exchanged trading rights for customs duties in 1854, while Kosoko's allies, such as Oshodi Tapa and Taiwo Olowo, traded freely with European firms.</p><p>Fearing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosoko">Kosoko</a> and the French at Wydah, the government of Lord Palmerston decided to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. On 30 July 1861 the acting consul William McCoskry and Commander Bedingfield explained the terms to Dosunmu aboard HMS Prometheus. He resisted until Bedingfield threatened violence against the town, then signed the treaty, which replaced his customs revenue with a pension of £1,000 a year. Later, French firms that had lost their concessions encouraged him to revolt against Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawley-glover">John Hawley Glover</a>, who put the attempt down, fined him £50 and suspended his stipend for four months.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His wealth declined after the cession, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosoko">Kosoko</a>'s faction, free of such trading restrictions, prospered; when Kosoko died, the colonial government judged his faction the stronger, with at least 20,000 followers. Dosunmu died in 1885 and was succeeded by his son, Oba Oyekan I. Among his descendants is Oloye Abiola Dosunmu, the Erelu Kuti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dosunmu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under pressure from Britain, Oba <b>Dosunmu</b> signed a treaty ceding Lagos in 1861, and it became Lagos Colony.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lagos">Kingdom of Lagos</a></li><li><blockquote>A British consul then dominated affairs, and in August 1861 Akitoye's successor <b>Dosunmu</b> signed a treaty ceding Lagos to Britain under pressure.</blockquote><a href="/state/lagos-colony">Lagos Colony</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lagos, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. Died in Lagos, in what is now <a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=6.46,3.39&amp;year=1875" aria-label="Lagos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4644 1922 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dosunmu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5094 2192h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=6.46,3.39&amp;year=1875">What was here in 1875? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
